\alias{PipeOpTorchRNN}
\title{Simple Recurrent Layer}
\description{
Applies a multi-layer Elman RNN with \eqn{\tanh} or \eqn{\mbox{ReLU}} non-linearity
to an input sequence.
}
\section{Tensor Layout}{

Input and output are \verb{(batch, sequence, feature)}, i.e. the \code{batch_first} layout of
\code{\link[torch:nn_rnn]{torch::nn_rnn()}}, which is fixed and not a hyperparameter.
\code{torch} defaults to \verb{(sequence, batch, feature)}, but the first dimension of every shape has to
be the batch dimension here.

The final hidden state is \verb{(layers * directions, batch, hidden_size)} in \code{torch}, even in the
batch-first layout. It is transposed to \verb{(batch, layers * directions, hidden_size)} here, so that
the batch dimension comes first for it as well.
}

\section{nn_module}{

Calls \code{\link[torch:nn_rnn]{torch::nn_rnn()}} when trained, where the parameter \code{input_size} is inferred as the last
dimension of the input tensor and \code{batch_first} is always \code{TRUE}, see section \emph{Tensor Layout}.
}

\section{Parameters}{

\itemize{
\item \code{hidden_size} :: \code{integer(1)}\cr
The number of features of the hidden state.
\item \code{num_layers} :: \code{integer(1)}\cr
The number of stacked recurrent layers. Default is \code{1}.
\item \code{bias} :: \code{logical(1)}\cr
Whether to use bias weights. Default is \code{TRUE}.
\item \code{dropout} :: \code{numeric(1)}\cr
Dropout probability on the output of each layer but the last. Only has an effect when
\code{num_layers} is greater than \code{1}. Default is \code{0}.
\item \code{bidirectional} :: \code{logical(1)}\cr
Whether to run the sequence in both directions and concatenate the results, which doubles the
size of the feature dimension of the output. Default is \code{FALSE}.
\item \code{nonlinearity} :: \code{character(1)}\cr
The activation function, either \code{"tanh"} or \code{"relu"}. Default is \code{"tanh"}.
}

Note that \code{input_size} is \emph{not} a parameter, as it is inferred from the shape of the input
tensor, and that \code{batch_first} is \emph{not} a parameter either, as it is fixed to \code{TRUE}, see section
\emph{Tensor Layout}.
}

\section{Input and Output Channels}{

There is one input channel \code{"input"}, the sequence to run over.

The output channels are determined by the construction argument \code{return_state}:
\itemize{
\item \code{return_state = FALSE} (default): one output channel \code{"output"}, the output sequence of shape
\verb{(batch, sequence, hidden_size * directions)}.
\item \code{return_state = TRUE}: an additional output channel \code{"h_n"} with the final hidden state, of
shape \verb{(batch, num_layers * directions, hidden_size)}.
}

For an explanation see \code{\link{PipeOpTorch}}.
}

\section{State}{

The state is the value calculated by the public method \verb{$shapes_out()}.
}

